<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Fast DDS profile for a HOST-side subscriber to the guest's RAS topics. Port 42105.

The guest's ras_monitor.py (../ras-ros/) publishes /ras/events and /diagnostics.
Nothing on the host can receive them today: a host participant is reachable from
the guest only if it owns a TCP acceptor, and the four that exist are taken.
42100 parameter_bridge, 42101 clock_decimator, 42102 cmd_vel_watchdog,
42103 rviz2, 42104 detection_overlay. This is the fifth.

Transport plumbing and nothing else. It names no topic and no type, so it stays
correct however the daemon's messages change, which matters because the daemon is
being written separately. What this file supplies is the part that is not the
daemon's choice: SLIRP only lets the guest dial out, and only to a port something
on the host is already listening on.

Two ways to watch the RAS topics during a run, and this file is the second.

  1. Over ssh, inside the guest. `ros2 topic echo /ras/events` on the guest's own
     profile crosses no DDS boundary and needs no port. Use this for a
     demonstration unless the message has to appear in a host-side tool.
  2. With this profile, on the host, when it does: rqt_robot_monitor, an RViz
     panel, or a `ros2 bag record` of the events beside the workload's topics.

The matching guest locator is generated, not committed: run_split.sh writes
guest_tcp_client_demo.xml from guest_tcp_client.xml when GUI=1 and includes 42105,
so the profile G3, G4 and every G5 were measured on stays byte-identical.

The guest side needs two variables the systemd unit in ../ras-ros/systemd/ does
not set, because that unit was written for a guest with no rig running:
ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42 and FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE pointing at the guest
profile. Without them the monitor comes up on domain 0 over shared memory, which
is a different ROS graph from the workload it exists to watch. See
../ras-ros/README.md and the RAS block in run_split.sh.

Load with:  export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/work/config/host_ras_server.xml
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>ras_server</transport_id>
        <type>TCPv4</type>
        <!-- The presence of listening_ports elects this side as the TCP server.
             42100 parameter_bridge, 42101 clock_decimator, 42102 cmd_vel_watchdog,
             42103 rviz2, 42104 detection_overlay, 42105 here. -->
        <listening_ports>
          <port>42105</port>
        </listening_ports>
        <!-- SLIRP routes exactly one address from the guest to this host. Without
             wan_addr the guest connects, discovers the participant, then has no
             reachable locator to match and sits silent. See host_tcp_server.xml. -->
        <wan_addr>10.0.2.2</wan_addr>
        <enable_tcp_nodelay>true</enable_tcp_nodelay>
        <keep_alive_frequency_ms>30000</keep_alive_frequency_ms>
        <keep_alive_timeout_ms>120000</keep_alive_timeout_ms>
        <calculate_crc>false</calculate_crc>
        <check_crc>false</check_crc>
        <max_logical_port>200</max_logical_port>
        <logical_port_range>50</logical_port_range>
        <!-- The defaults left every topic above ~700 B silently undelivered on
             this link (host_tcp_server.xml). a DiagnosticArray of RAS
             KeyValues is a few hundred bytes, so size is not the reason these are
             here. They match the sibling profiles because a link that behaves
             differently from its siblings is a debugging trap. -->
        <maxMessageSize>65000</maxMessageSize>
        <sendBufferSize>1048576</sendBufferSize>
        <receiveBufferSize>1048576</receiveBufferSize>
      </transport_descriptor>

      <!-- Explicit, because the builtin transports are off below and the viewer
           still has to reach the bridge in the OTHER container. Declaring UDPv4
           by hand gives multicast discovery and host-local data delivery without
           bringing shared memory back. -->
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>udp_local</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="vela_host_ras" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>ras_server</transport_id>
          <transport_id>udp_local</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- false, and this is the one place in the rig where false is right.
             The viewer runs in a SECOND container. Docker gives it a private IPC
             namespace and therefore its own /dev/shm, so a shared-memory locator
             announced by either side names a segment the other cannot open.
             Measured 2026-08-19: see host_rviz_server.xml, same container, same
             result. Turning the builtin set off removes SHM from the choices;
             the explicit UDPv4 descriptor above keeps everything SHM was doing.
             The 08-17 decision to flip host_tcp_server.xml back to true does not
             apply here: that participant is in the same container as the bridge,
             and it had no explicit UDP transport to fall back on. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>

    <!-- Same block as host_tcp_server.xml, kept for symmetry rather than for a
         measured effect: rmw_fastrtps sets publish mode from
         RMW_FASTRTPS_PUBLICATION_MODE and ignores an XML data_writer profile
         unless RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML is set, which nothing here sets.
         The viewer publishes almost nothing anyway. -->
    <data_writer profile_name="vela_ras_async" is_default_profile="true">
      <qos>
        <publishMode>
          <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
        </publishMode>
      </qos>
    </data_writer>

  </profiles>
</dds>
